        </details>
      </nav>
      <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The G7 leaders agreed on October 2 to a coordinated release through the International Energy Agency of 100 million barrels over four months, beginning immediately, with what their statement calls "a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days." It gives no number for that diesel. The leaders also agreed to stagger maintenance at their refineries so they do not shut down at the same time, to run them harder where feasible, and not to restrict energy exports to one another. The leaders set the release "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" and did not say how much of the 100 million barrels is new beyond the 400 million barrels International Energy Agency members pledged in March.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The leaders said the measures are meant to "shield households and businesses from price shocks." Prices have climbed since the US-Israeli war with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine disrupted global markets. U.S. retail diesel cost {prices.dieselSep28} dollars a gallon on September 28, up from {prices.dieselMar2} on March 2. In Europe, the European Commission said diesel supply "remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high." In Washington, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said American farmers, truckers and businesses "should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage."<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="shield households and businesses from price shocks" /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /><Cite s="ec-taskforce-2026-10-02" passage="EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The March release was not finished. By October 2 about 325 million of its 400 million barrels had been released. The United States was still carrying out its 172-million-barrel share by lending crude from its reserve to companies, which repay the loans later with extra barrels. Earlier offers had awarded more than 133 million barrels, and on September 29 it offered up to 40 million more. Washington was also pressing Europe for more. On September 30 President Donald Trump said he was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports, and on October 1 Bessent wrote that "our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available." The leaders agreed to the new release the next day, with a pledge to refrain from export restrictions on energy among the seven countries, and hours later Trump said he would not authorize an export ban on diesel.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`still "thinking about" banning diesel exports`} /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>March pledge and what followed</strong><span>The agency members' March pledge, the barrels released by October 2, and the United States share and exchange.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Release", "Barrels"]} rows={volumeRows} />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Japan has no plans at this stage for another release from its reserves. "Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage," Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara said on October 5. In March Japan said it would release about 80 million barrels from its reserves as its part of the coordinated agreement.<Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="Japanese Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage" /><Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="In March, Japan said it would release about 80 million barrels of oil from its reserves as part of the coordinated agreement." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">In March the agency said the overall release would largely consist of crude oil, while Europe's contributions would be mostly refined products, and that split was subject to change. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells, and diesel is what has run short. Net exports of diesel and gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of their level before the war with Iran, and Gulf and Russian exports together were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February. On September 30 Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because conflicts involving Russia and the Middle East have cut exports, and that American refiners are running at record highs.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East`} /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The October statement turns to refining too. The leaders will coordinate refinery maintenance, run refineries harder where feasible, and encourage countries with large refining capacity to produce more refined products, particularly diesel. They call for navigation rights through the Strait of Hormuz to be restored, and the French presidency said shipments through Hormuz and through Yanbu on the Red Sea had been rising in recent days.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The G7 leaders' statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and "temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible", and "We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel".<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /></p>
          <p>The same statement says the release takes into account "commitments that have already been fulfilled," asks the International Energy Agency to monitor the March 2026 commitments, and says the group will meet again "to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." It also says the members "reaffirm our commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries," and that a follow-up report "should be delivered before 20 days." It says the report should "include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." It does not say 20 days from when, or who delivers the report. It calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days, include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency published the same English statement, including the {release.barrels} barrel release over {release.months} and the diesel release "within the first 20 days."<Cite s="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On March 11, Chris Wright said 32 member nations agreed to a "coordinated release of 400 million barrels," and that the United States would "release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week," over "approximately 120 days." He said the United States had arranged to replace the draw with "approximately 200 million barrels within the next year."<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The March 11 G7 presidency communique says leaders welcomed the International Energy Agency announcement, prepared at the G7's initiative, "de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques", to release up to 400 million barrels of strategic reserves.<Cite s="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" passage="de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Attending the meeting were President Donald Trump, Chancellor Friedrich Merz, Prime Minister Mark Carney, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni, European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="The meeting was attended by President Donald Trump of the United States, Chancellor Friedrich Merz of Germany, Prime Minister Mark Carney of Canada, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy, President Ursula von der Leyen of the European Commission, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband was to stand in for Andy Burnham at the talks while the Prime Minister attended his father's funeral.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband will stand in for Andy Burnham during urgent G7 talks regarding global energy markets, as the Prime Minister attends his father’s funeral." /></p>
          <p>British fuel prices had hit £2 a litre for the first time. The previous record, 199.09p, was set in June 2022.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time" /><Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="The previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency's readout says the G7 leaders decided unanimously. It refers to a letter from President Macron to the president of the European Commission about running refineries at full capacity, and says first measures had already been decided at European level.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Ils ont décidé, à l'unanimité :" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="la lettre que le Président de la République avait envoyée à la Présidente de la Commission européenne" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The International Energy Agency's March 19 contributions page describes the 11 March agreement as one "to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market". It says the overall release "will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It calls the action "the sixth time" members have acted together since the agency "was created in 1974," after actions "in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022," and says the war in the Middle East is creating "the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market." Executive Director Fatih Birol's remarks are named on that page. The saved contributions workbook reports a "Total IEA" of 426.03075080054771 in the column marked (mb). That total is not a second release and not barrels delivered.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="The war in the Middle East is creating the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-workbook-2026-03-19" passage="Total IEA,426.03075080054771" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
          <summary>English statement and French readout</summary>
          <DataTable columns={["Record", "Wording of the 100 million barrels"]} rows={statementRows} />
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>A diesel release, and no number for it</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The joint statement promises "a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" and names no diesel amount and no country that will supply the barrels. Before the meeting, according to one source, the United States had demanded that large European countries, including France and Germany, release 100 million barrels of diesel within 20 days. Earlier that day European Union countries discussed a French proposal for 50 million barrels of diesel from Europe and 50 million barrels of crude from International Energy Agency members. Each account described the 100 million barrels its own way. The French presidency's readout says up to 100 million barrels within four months, of refined products, diesel and crude. The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks." Moments before the statement was released, Trump posted that "Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." On October 5 the White House said he had negotiated with Europe "to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." None of these accounts reports a barrel delivered.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="In October 2026, President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /><Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Moments before the statement was released, US President Donald Trump said in a social media post" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="The US had demanded large European countries including France and Germany release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European Union countries discussed a French proposal on Friday to release diesel stocks" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="wsj-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">A post on X by Eric Daugherty the same day went further: "100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins," the agriculture secretary. Her own words in a television interview that day were more hedged.<Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Early in the interview Rollins spoke of "100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon." She added, "I think they’re moving out right now." She does not call the barrels diesel in that passage. The post dropped her hedge, stated the barrels as already en route, and added diesel. Asked later about a diesel export ban, she said, "I’m the secretary of agriculture, not energy," and that she is not in those conversations.<Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-interview-captions-2026-10-02" passage="I’m the secretary of agriculture, not energy, and I’m I’m certainly not in any of these conversations." /><Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /></p>

            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Oil reserve decisions</strong><span>March to October 2026.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={chronology} />
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Diesel and crude</strong><span>Weekly U.S. retail diesel, Brent (the Europe crude price), and WTI (West Texas Intermediate, a U.S. crude price).</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Series", "Observation", "Dollars"]} rows={priceRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="gasdesw-2026-10-03" kind="computed" detail>
          <p>Diesel is {prices.dieselFromMarch} dollars a gallon, or {prices.dieselFromMarchPercent} percent, above March 2. Brent, the Europe crude price, is {prices.brentFromFebruary} dollars a barrel above February 27. WTI, West Texas Intermediate, is {prices.wtiFromFebruary} dollars above February 27.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,71.32" /><Cite s="dcoilwtico-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,66.96" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far prices have moved</strong><span>The change in weekly diesel since September 21 and since March 2, and the change in Brent and WTI since February 27.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Comparison", "Change", "Unit"]} rows={changeRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 29 the Energy Department issued a request for an "exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, continuing its execution of the 172 million barrel release announced in March. Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. Secretary Chris Wright said several European member countries "have released only a fraction" of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged. The department said five earlier solicitations "collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels," with a "25 percent premium in returned barrels." Bids are due "October 6, 2026." Deliveries under those awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" kind="record" detail>
          <p>U.S. stocks of distillate fuel oil were {distillate.sep25} thousand barrels in the week of September 25, down from {distillate.sep18} thousand the week before.<Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="Stocks of Distillate Fuel Oil" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="(Thousand Barrels)" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="08/21/26 08/28/26 09/04/26 09/11/26 09/18/26 09/25/26 View History U.S. 103,391 104,187 106,274 107,859 107,431 105,180" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The German government's energy FAQ says the 32 IEA members agreed in March to release "400 Millionen Barrel (gut 54 Millionen Tonnen)," and that "Deutschland hat die Freigabe am 18. März erteilt." It says Germany's emergency reserve "für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen," and that the IEA had asked Germany to release "2,6 Millionen Tonnen."<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Deutschlands Notfallreserve für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /></p>
          <p>The same FAQ says market participants have since called up a first tranche of the amount set for release, and that the market did not consider a further release necessary.<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On Friday evening the German government welcomed the G7 decision to release 100 million barrels of oil and oil products such as diesel.<Cite s="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" passage="Auch die Bundesregierung hat den G-7-Beschluss zur Freigabe von 100 Millionen Barrel Öl und Ölprodukten wie Diesel als wichtiges Zeichen für die Handlungsfähigkeit und Geschlossenheit der Gruppe begrüßt." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 30 the Russian government extended its ban on diesel exports by producers until October 31.<Cite s="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" passage="The Russian government has extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel, ship fuel and gasoil for producers, which was set to expire today, until October 31" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 2 Russia said it would consider a partial lifting of its diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction. Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said the domestic diesel market is currently balanced.<Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="Russia said on Friday it would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction" /><Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="the domestic diesel market is currently balanced" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserve held less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982. U.S. law bars the president from ordering routine, small drawdowns if it falls below 252.4 million barrels. An earlier offer drew agreement to borrow only about 500,000 barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="The amount of oil in US SPR has sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982." /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels." /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

      </section>

      <section id="export-ban">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The export ban</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A source in a European capital described a larger U.S. ask: 120 million barrels of diesel from the European Union over the next six months. One European diplomat called the White House’s demands for a release, set against the threat of a U.S. export ban, “blackmail.”<Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /><Cite s="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" passage="One European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail,”" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Hours after the agreement, Trump said he would not authorize an export ban on diesel. "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing," he said. French President Emmanuel Macron said Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on U.S. diesel.<Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The European Commission rejected any ban. "We fully reject any ban on diesel," spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said. Commission President Ursula von der Leyen welcomed the outcome on X.<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel." /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Ursula von der Leyen, European Commission president" when="October 2, 2026" where="X post, via RTÉ" label="RTÉ">
          <p>We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks.<Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies" /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 16, 2026, Mr. Burchett and Mr. Fuller introduced H.R. 10422, "To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during certain periods, and for other purposes." The bill tells the Secretary of Commerce to prohibit diesel exports for a period that begins on the fourteenth consecutive day the average retail price is greater than $5 a gallon, and ends on the first day after that start which is the thirtieth consecutive day the price is less than $4.50 a gallon. The text refers the bill to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="Secretary of Commerce shall prohibit the export of diesel fuel" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="begins on the date that is the fourteenth consecutive" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="greater than $5 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="thirtieth consecutive day on which" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="less than $4.50 per gallon" /><Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="which was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The same day, the same sponsors introduced H.R. 10423. It says "the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited" from enactment through "December 31, 2026." It too was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited during the period beginning on the date of the enactment of this subsection and ending on the date December 31, 2026." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban, an RBC note says. On September 22 the president said, "I've said let's not send out the diesel."<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban" /><Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 23 Javier Blas wrote that industry groups had sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits on diesel exports.<Cite s="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" passage="have sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits of diesels exports." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Ohio is suspending its state gas and diesel taxes for 90 days.<Cite s="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" passage="Ohio is implementing a 90-day state gas and diesel tax suspension." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Commission spokesperson said a U.S. ban "would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." Von der Leyen said, "Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy." Since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled.<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Rollins also said, "We are in the middle of harvest season," and, "For the short term, we're going to do everything we can."<Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="We are in the middle of harvest season" /><Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="For the short term, we're going to do everything we can" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What comes next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The leaders will meet again through the International Energy Agency "in the coming days to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." They invite the agency to monitor the impact of the measures and whether the commitments are carried out, and a follow-up report "should be delivered before 20 days" and "include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will convene in the context of the IEA in the coming days to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On October 5 Trump also signed an order on diesel taxes, saying "restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices." If the Treasury secretary determines which taxpayers qualify, it tells him to defer, to the extent authorized by law, their payment of specified federal excise taxes incurred from October 5 through December 31, 2026. Within five days he is to have the Internal Revenue Service announce it will not impose a penalty when dyed diesel, the fuel sold for off-road use, is used on the highway in that period.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="Restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="If the Secretary makes those determinations, the Secretary shall, to the extent authorized by law, defer payment by those taxpayers of the taxes imposed by 26 U.S.C. 4041(a)(1)(A) or by 26 U.S.C. 4041(b)(1)(B) and that are incurred during the period of October 5, 2026, through December 31, 2026." /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="to announce that it will not impose a penalty" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-tax-order-2026-10-05" passage="when dyed diesel fuel is sold for use or used on the highway during the period of October 5, 2026, through December 31, 2026" /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="Today, President Donald J. Trump signed an Executive Order to temporarily allow off-road “dyed” diesel for highway use and defer the applicable Federal excise tax" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Bids on the September 29 exchange offer were due October 6, and companies that borrow the barrels will return them later with extra barrels. Earlier solicitations had already awarded more than 133 million barrels, with deliveries under those exchanges scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="October 6, 2026" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6" data-absence>The joint text does not say how much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, which countries will supply it, or how it splits between diesel and crude. On October 5 the White House described it as 100 million barrels of refined diesel from Europe's strategic reserves within four months.<Cite s="reuters-japan-no-release-2026-10-05" passage="It was uncertain how much of the new release would come from what remains from the 400 million barrels of oil that the 32 countries that are members of the International Energy Agency agreed to release in March to mitigate disruptions from the US-Israeli war on Iran." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="produits raffinés, notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="In October 2026, President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 2 U.S. oil prices dropped as much as 5 percent at one point. Brent settled down 6 cents, at $102.25 a barrel, and WTI finished down $1.76, at $91.11.<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="Brent settled down 6 cents, or 0.06%, at $102.25 a barrel." /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /><Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="WTI finished down $1.76, or 1.90%, at $91.11 a barrel." /></p>
          <p>A remark quoted on October 2 put 80 percent of overall International Energy Agency stocks “still in our pocket.” That share counts all emergency stocks; the share of March's 400 million barrels already released is a separate figure.<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="80 percent of overall IEA stocks “still in our pocket”" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas wrote on October 2 that the price of U.S. wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel was back to its level of five weeks earlier.<Cite s="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" passage="The price of US wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel is back to the level of five weeks ago." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas, an energy and commodities columnist at Bloomberg,<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Energy and commodities columnist at Bloomberg." /> wrote on X that questions about whether the release includes March barrels that were not released, or were bought back, are "a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now."<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Javier Blas on X" /><Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="Lots of questions about whether the G7 oil / diesel release includes barrels from March that were not released (and/or bought back). It's a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

